A||N||7||fropuff||Can someone settle this for us?||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||09-13-2000||06:27 PM||||Hey, me and my brother are arguing about something.  He has a buyer for his '92 Grand Prix SE, and he is in the process of looking for a 95 Camaro Z28.  He found one in the MN Autotrader, and he likes it a lot.  It's a 95, white, T-tops, leather, 148,000 miles.  I was saying to him that it's stupid to buy a car with that many miles on it.  His response is, and I quote:  "It's as much as the truck has."  We have a 96 Chevrolet Silverado with 165K miles.  I said that trucks can handle that many miles.  I know that cars can last for 200,000 but he says he'll be happy if it lasts 20K.  He also sayd that cars last LONGER than trucks do.  I totally disagree with him on this.  Does anyone else have some remarks?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||09-13-2000||06:46 PM||||Lets see... 5 year old car 148000 miles, must be highway milage, (30000 per year) not too harmful if the oil was changed routinely.<P>I've seen more high milage trucks than high milage cars.  Trucks keep going after they have lost their body dynamics. (rust, body damage etc)  While cars tend to be repaired or trashed when they get beat, trucks just keep on rolling on.<P>Me.. I wouldn't touch a 148000 mile car, but if he only expects 20000 more milage (probably a realistic expectation) and the car is not costing too much, let him spend the money.||||reg||4
Z||000002||Cooter||09-13-2000||07:03 PM||||If the car was cheap enough, I'd take a chance. But if the previous owner(s) did not take car of the car, then it may be a risk not worth taking. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-13-2000||09:34 PM||||Be afraid.  Be very afraid.<P>I'd be worried about a high mileage performance car.  A Honda (don't flame me for saying the "H" word) probably gets pretty easy miles, but a Camaro...?  How many hard starts and fast corners are included in the miles.<P>Be afraid.  Be very afraid.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||09-13-2000||10:50 PM||||Exactly my opinion, Monkeyman. It all comes down to how the vehicle was driven and maintained. I think this applies to both cars and trucks so just a generic comparison of car vs truck wouldn't be fair.||||reg||1
Z||000005||frontal lobe||09-14-2000||01:48 AM||||My experience is trucks get more miles than cars.<P>You don't get a z28 to baby it.  The miles are probably harder than the average car.  Unless the price was awesome, I'd look elsewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||09-14-2000||01:55 AM||||the body, and suspension on trucks are probably better built and longer lasting.<P>Most trucks come with the same engines as cars, and would break down at the same rate.<P>Before you start up the car, look if all the fluids are filled.  No leaks.  No blue smoke startup, oil in the filter.  Compression check if you really wanna be careful.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||09-14-2000||05:33 AM||||I'd have an independant mechanic take a look at it.  He can't make any guarantees, but at least he can give you some idea of what things are like.  It would be $50 well spent.  (Just make sure it's not the sellers brother-in-law or something. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
